{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213107",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- Let's consider the first components of the pairs belonging to our relation:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(-5,1,(-5,2),(0,3),(8,1),(8,5)\\}\n$$\n\nThe set of first components is $\\{-5,-5,0,8,8\\}=\\{-5,0,8\\}.$ \nHence, the domain is $\\{-5,0,8\\}.$\n\n- Let's consider the second components of the pairs belonging to our relation:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(-5,1),(-5,2),(0,3),(8,1),(8,5)\\}\n$$\n\nThe set of second components is $\\{1,2,3,1,5\\}=\\{1,2,3,5\\}.$ \nHence, the range is $\\{1,2,3,5\\}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
